<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      The application's version, and the only place it is written.

      Every project under this file takes it from here, so the application, each module library and the on-host
      service all report one version rather than one each. The SDK derives AssemblyVersion (1.1.0.0), FileVersion
      and InformationalVersion from it, which is what the Settings screen's App Version line, the diagnostics
      context recorded on every log entry, and the service's reported version all read.

      Two declarations cannot be derived and are kept in step by hand on each bump, because each is read by a
      different loader before any managed code runs:
        - Package.appxmanifest  -> the MSIX identity version (four parts)
        - app.manifest, MTM_Waitlist.Mock.Service/app.manifest -> the native assembly identity
    -->
    <Version>1.1.0</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(AppxPackage)'=='false'">
    <WindowsAppSDKDeploymentManagerInitialize>false</WindowsAppSDKDeploymentManagerInitialize>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- WinUIEx exposes its obsolete Icon converter through generated XAML metadata. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0618</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      NETSDK1206 ("Found version-specific or distribution-specific runtime identifier(s)") fires on a
      COLD restore of every module library because the transitive Microsoft.Graphics.Win2D 1.0.0.30
      package ships assets under the version-specific RIDs win10-x64 / win10-x86 / win10-arm64, while
      these projects declare the portable RIDs win-x64 / win-x86 / win-arm64. .NET 8+ uses a smaller
      portable RID graph by default, so it no longer resolves those version-specific assets.

      Resolved by restoring the full RID graph, which is the resolution Microsoft documents for
      NETSDK1206 ("If you need to revert to the previous behavior of using the old, full RID graph,
      you can set the UseRidGraph MSBuild property to true"):
      https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/tools/sdk-errors/netsdk1206

      This keeps the severity of the real signal intact for every other warning — it is a RID
      resolution setting, not a NoWarn suppression. Verified: a clean restore now builds with
      0 warnings / 0 errors.

      ROOT-CAUSE ALTERNATIVE (verified, not taken here): Win2D 1.4.0 ships portable win-* assets and
      would remove the need for this flag entirely (its only constraint is
      Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK.WinUI >= 1.8.260204000, satisfied by the 2.3.1 in use). It is not taken
      as part of this change because Win2D is transitive via CommunityToolkit.WinUI.UI.* 7.1.2, which
      was compiled against Win2D 1.0.0.30 — changing that assembly version is a runtime binding risk
      that neither the build nor the test suite can validate. Schedule it as a separate change with
      UI/render validation, then remove this property.

      Note: the old RID graph is not updated for new distributions/architectures and may be removed in
      a future .NET release; this property is therefore a tracked accommodation, not a permanent fix.
    -->
    <UseRidGraph>true</UseRidGraph>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Security pin, not a direct dependency of anything here.

      Microsoft.Data.SqlClient 6.1.1 resolves System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs 9.0.4 transitively in every
      project, and that version carries two advisories: GHSA-846f-f764-76mx (high) and GHSA-2fmv-rv2c-59x7
      (moderate). NuGet's audit reports them as NU1903 / NU1902 on every restore, once per project, which is
      the 56-warning build this pin removes.

      The vulnerable ranges are 8.0.0-8.0.1, 9.0.0-9.0.20 and 10.0.0-10.0.12 (read from the advisory data,
      not from memory). 9.0.21 is the lowest release outside all three, and it stays in the major that
      SqlClient was compiled against, so no assembly binding changes: the smallest safe change, not a jump to
      10.x.

      Pinned here rather than in the four projects that reference SqlClient, because Pkcs is transitive and
      the pin has to hold wherever it resolves. Remove this when SqlClient itself requires a patched Pkcs;
      removing it sooner restores NU1902 and NU1903 on the next restore.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs" Version="9.0.21" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
